# proxy-metric-validation-antipattern

Using indirect signals — version counters, single-layer checks, surface-level status codes — as stand-ins for real correctness validation produces false greens. The proxy can succeed while the underlying invariant silently fails. Validation must always target the actual invariant directly: real content retrievability, all coupled layers agreeing, the substantive outcome — not a metric that merely correlates with it.

## Procedures
- Identify the true invariant that must hold (e.g. 'content is actually retrievable', 'all coupled layers are consistent').
- Assert directly on that invariant, not on a metric that usually correlates with it (e.g. a version counter, a top-level status code).
- When multiple layers must agree, write assertions that exercise every layer, not just the outermost or most convenient one.
- After any refactor or integration change, re-verify that assertions still target the real invariant and have not drifted to proxy signals.

